#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050259 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050259 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050259 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050259 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050259 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050259 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050259 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050259 #edb/ref 1031 "Limits at Infinity from Graphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050259 #edb/ref 1033 1873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050259 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3a3e1a52-75c9-5495-b430-05a58e4d5dde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050274 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050274 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050274 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050274 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050274 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050274 #edb/ref 1031 "The Finite Limit of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050274 #edb/ref 1033 461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050274 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3bdc5470-2e0b-5f3c-9875-854353ddeb19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050454 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050454 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050454 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050454 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050454 #edb/ref 1031 "The Left and Right-Sided Limits of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050454 #edb/ref 1033 472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050454 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4a48339a-3719-56ed-a805-f01876009733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068428 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068428 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/eead78a813f820cf12cbd05bd22b230b1753c7e4ebe1e77d2c1eb6b61c11f4ed.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068428 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "282add39-5184-589e-bb7b-b87a59cd41e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068428 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068533 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068533 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0} f(x)$ for the function below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0ed78e2e640611d70263e17a76eb4a13e9e2816efba02494b53e540d38065d6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068533 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb401fbe-a718-5ea7-b598-139ef837b047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068533 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $0,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)= 1\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(0)$ is $2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068534 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068534 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below? \n\nI. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0$\n\nIII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)$\n\n![](images/68/68241f3cd913cd91d10765499dc4ce4398fb3e8a81ec4bcfb65b47c0d77731d6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab95d805-958c-5de3-99bd-41368cc57080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068534 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $3,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSimiliarly, as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)\\ne\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Limit When the Function Value is Not Defined" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068791 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "607443d5-b1dd-56b1-919b-fcc652ac3bc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068792 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Limit When the Limit is Not Equal to the Function Value" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068792 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068792 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e5fe1482-00d6-5738-b56a-8c082912d8d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068795 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068795 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068795 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068795 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068795 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068795 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About Limits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068795 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068795 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "851c841f-6e27-5f81-8a0d-0f2439ae3e61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068988 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068988 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068988 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068988 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068988 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068988 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068988 #edb/ref 1045 "The Finite Limit of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068988 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068988 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068988 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068988 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c2e3d487-c981-55e6-ba25-8949d030cfa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069709 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069709 #edb/ref 1061 256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069709 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "309b25fa-6c05-5421-8f80-e956e2975682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069709 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069713 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069713 #edb/ref 1061 257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069713 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01fbff48-2ebc-5e33-a1b9-e271896f5340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069713 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069714 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069714 #edb/ref 1061 260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069714 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9144fd7b-81d4-5cb5-8051-0619e464002c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069714 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069717 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069717 #edb/ref 1061 2621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069717 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d21275b2-0542-5145-8c14-535a8c0db329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069717 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069718 #edb/ref 1061 263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069718 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0447b7b5-01f2-52cd-851f-84ee0d719836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069718 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069924 #edb/ref 1064 "The limit of a function at some point has **nothing to do** with the value of the function at that point.\n\nFor example, the following functions $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ have *different values* at $x=2,$ but they have the *same limit* at $x=2.$\n\n![](images/6a/6a73b74072a8a990c91a38553d519f4913370d34ef0251b7e45cc664da40517d.png)\n\n![](images/06/06e150aacae774b4fe883a2750ef13cebbddf2ea871e5a682f2587c47a54615a.png)\n\nThe actual values of $g(2)$ and $h(2)$ are irrelevant for determining the limit at $x=2$. All that matters is what values the functions *look like* they are approaching as $x$ approaches $2$. \n\nNear $x=2,$ both $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ appear to be approaching $y=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}g(x)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}h(x)=1 \\, .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069924 #edb/ref 1065 1333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069924 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ea300af0-adfe-5df2-8b4d-ae23430245a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069924 #edb/ref 1067 "Limit Has Nothing to Do With the Function Value" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069928 #edb/ref 1064 "The **limit** of a function is the value that the function approaches. For example, the function $y=f(x)$ shown below is not defined at $x=2,$ as represented by the open circle there $-$ but when we *approach* $x=2$, we see that $f(x)$ *approaches* the value $y=1.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7081d649c6cdfa0a19a0fb2de93b97ee700ad922f34530c7f3e5c2a3228c9fb.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$, the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ In other words, the limit of $f(x),$ as $x$ approaches $2,$ is $1$. We can express this mathematically as \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)=1 \\, .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069928 #edb/ref 1065 138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069928 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e52528a2-ed6a-5d07-89d4-1a74d70eb6b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069928 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513527 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513527 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513527 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00825b34-ca14-5ae2-ab19-c20bf17e8d48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513557 #edb/ref 1