366 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bebd627-ffbe-5f9e-9279-43f33938aacb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514366 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514371 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514371 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514371 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514371 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a4/a45bf16dabedfdd0c8f4569289c56a5f89c127e240f57a8b9259429be86a9cde.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514371 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1580d634-b7c9-566e-bd94-0e3785439c42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514371 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514434 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335759" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514434 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514434 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/22/22acfe14709e4992ddadf6d1d4fcd0e2a533c93573c32dd4134ec3498b0ea805.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87458c76-eabf-5005-ac30-a442db3ab7b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514434 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $-3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(2)$ is $-2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514440 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514440 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514440 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514440 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7d/7defdf7c31a164fdbe702c1f5e87567f5da868d7530143493f9713ff88de98bc.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)$ for the function above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514440 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9722b50e-b540-58f7-8d1d-e4a47908bdb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514440 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(-\\frac{3}{2})$ is $0.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514441 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514441 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514441 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c3/c336dbdbda8ea15f5d2e86eb75493690d11ad0080c3ceeac9f71ab48cb63737b.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514441 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c2e4638-f161-548b-867c-b6be12db26ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514441 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514469 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514469 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34927" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514469 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514469 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/67/6769c6590446a9599786758ba9cfaf17690f401d864b3ce5f2c3825d9ca432cd.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514469 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e220bff6-4f48-5639-8c40-6889828b8e5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514469 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0,$ and consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n As a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514479 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514479 #edb/ref 1069 "q-300752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514479 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514479 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/93/93fc83d79679ca9f2b58729af1e3e87c2a01c19c12bc97670fbff398d63d061d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514479 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5f74f57-aaaa-5a36-a7e2-f4e15eb6a61c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514479 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(2)$ is $-2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
